# Apple HIG UI Foundation

When designing, building, or changing UI in the Lune V6 dashboard, treat Apple HIG as the foundation.

## Required

1. Load Apple HIG guidance first (`~/.codex/skills/apple-hig/SKILL.md` + relevant distilled topics: accessibility, buttons, text-fields, layout, color, typography).
2. Touch targets: iOS default **44×44 pt** (min 28×28). Prefer `--control-height` (44px). Do not make controls needlessly larger than 44pt.
3. Contrast: meet WCAG AA (4.5:1 normal text / 3:1 large or bold). Support light and dark.
4. Never convey status with color alone — pair with label, icon, or shape.
5. Match existing Lune tokens (`--accent`, `--control-*`, `--text-*`, `--separator`, `--surface-*`) and shared kit classes (`.ui-btn`, `.ui-input`, `.ui-select`, `.ui-row`).
6. Compact mobile density: 44pt is enough; tighten padding before growing hit areas.
7. Zone chrome: static overview cards may be multi-line (≥56–60px). Mobile zone chips stay single-line at `--control-height` (44px) without temp/status.

## Do not

- Invent oversized “chunky” controls, heavy multi-layer shadows, or gradient button chrome that fights the kit
- Cram temp/status into the mobile bottom zone picker — keep that condensed; put rich status in the page overview
- Ship generic AI aesthetics (purple gradients, cream+terracotta broadsheet, glow pills, emoji decoration)

## Examples

```css
/* ❌ BAD — oversized form control */
.ui-input { min-height: 56px; padding: 14px 16px; }

/* ✅ GOOD — HIG default via token */
.ui-input {
  height: var(--control-height, 44px);
  min-height: var(--control-height, 44px);
  padding: 0 10px;
}
```
